.SH NAME
frontend/node_modules/use-callback-ref Directory Reference
.SH SYNOPSIS
.br
.PP
.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 
 
.SH "🤙 use-callback-ref 📞"
.PP

.PP
 
.br
 The same \fRuseRef\fP but it will callback: 📞 Hello! Your ref was changed! 
.br
 \fR \fP \fR \fP \fR \fP 

.PP
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
Keep in mind that useRef doesn't notify you when its content changes\&. Mutating the \&.current property doesn't cause a re-render\&. If you want to run some code when React attaches or detaches a ref to a DOM node, you may want to use a callback ref instead \&.\&.\&.\&. \fBuseCallbackRef\fP instead\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
– \fRHooks API Reference\fP

.PP
Read more about \fRuse-callback\fP pattern and use cases:

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
https://dev.to/thekashey/the-same-useref-but-it-will-callback-8bo
.PP

.PP
This library exposes helpers to handle any case related to \fRref\fP \fIlifecycle\fP

.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRuseCallbackRef\fP - react on a ref change (replacement for \fRuseRef\fP)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRcreateCallbackRef\fP - - low level version of \fRuseCallbackRef\fP
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRuseMergeRefs\fP - merge multiple refs together creating a stable return ref
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRmergeRefs\fP - low level version of \fRuseMergeRefs\fP
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRuseTransformRef\fP - transform one ref to another (replacement for \fRuseImperativeHandle\fP)
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRtransformRef\fP - low level version of \fRuseTransformRef\fP
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRuseRefToCallback\fP - convert RefObject to an old callback-style ref
.IP "  \(bu" 4
\fRrefToCallback\fP - low level version of \fRuseRefToCallback\fP
.PP

.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRassignRef\fP - assign value to the ref, regardless it is RefCallback or RefObject
.PP

.PP
All functions are tree shakable, but even together it's \fBless then 300b\fP\&.
.SH "API"
.PP
💡 Some commands are hooks based, and returns the same refs/functions every render\&. But some are not, to be used in classes or non-react code\&.
.SS "useRef API"
🤔 Use case: every time you have to react to ref change

.PP
API is 99% compatible with React \fRcreateRef\fP and \fRuseRef\fP, and just adds another argument - \fRcallback\fP, which would be called on \fBref update\fP\&.
.SS "createCallbackRef - to replace React\&.createRef"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRcreateCallbackRef(callback)\fP - would call provided \fRcallback\fP when ref is changed\&.
.PP
.SS "useCallbackRef - to replace React\&.useRef"
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRuseCallbackRef(initialValue, callback)\fP - would call provided \fRcallback\fP when ref is changed\&.
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
\fRcallback\fP in both cases is \fRcallback(newValue, oldValue)\fP\&. Callback would not be called if newValue and oldValue is the same\&. 
.RE
.PP

.PP
.PP
.nf
import { useRef, createRef, useState } from 'react';
import { useCallbackRef, createCallbackRef } from 'use\-callback\-ref';

const Component = () => {
  const [, forceUpdate] = useState();
  // I dont need callback when ref changes
  const ref = useRef(null);

  // but sometimes \- it could be what you need
  const anotherRef = useCallbackRef(null, () => forceUpdate());

  useEffect(() => {
    // now it's just possible
  }, [anotherRef\&.current]); // react to dom node change
};
.fi
.PP

.PP
💡 You can use \fRuseCallbackRef\fP to convert RefObject into RefCallback, creating bridges between the old and the new code

.PP
.PP
.nf
// some old component
const onRefUpdate = (newRef) => {\&.\&.\&.}
const refObject = useCallbackRef(null, onRefUpdate);
// \&.\&.\&.
<SomeNewComponent ref={refObject}/>
.fi
.PP
.SS "assignRef"
🤔 Use case: every time you need to assign ref manually, and you dont know the shape of the ref

.PP
\fRassignRef(ref, value)\fP - assigns \fRvalues\fP to the \fRref\fP\&. \fRref\fP could be RefObject or RefCallback\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
🚫 ref\&.current = value // what if it's a callback\-ref?
🚫 ref(value) // but what if it's a object ref?

import {assignRef} from "use\-callback\-ref";
✅ assignRef(ref, value);
.fi
.PP
.SS "useTransformRef (to replace React\&.useImperativeHandle)"
🤔 Use case: ref could be different\&. \fRtransformRef(ref, tranformer):Ref\fP - return a new \fRref\fP which would propagate all changes to the provided \fRref\fP with applied \fRtransform\fP

.PP
.PP
.nf
// before
const ResizableWithRef = forwardRef((props, ref) => <Resizable {\&.\&.\&.props} ref={(i) => i && ref(i\&.resizable)} />);

// after

const ResizableWithRef = forwardRef((props, ref) => (
  <Resizable {\&.\&.\&.props} ref={transformRef(ref, (i) => (i ? i\&.resizable : null))} />
));
.fi
.PP
.SS "refToCallback"
\fRrefToCallback(ref: RefObject): RefCallback\fP - for compatibility between the old and the new code\&. For the compatibility between \fRRefCallback\fP and RefObject use \fRuseCallbackRef(undefined, callback)\fP
.SS "useMergeRefs"
\fRmergeRefs(refs: arrayOfRefs, [defaultValue]):ReactMutableRef\fP - merges a few refs together

.PP
When developing low level UI components, it is common to have to use a local ref but also support an external one using React\&.forwardRef\&. Natively, React does not offer a way to set two refs inside the ref property\&. This is the goal of this small utility\&.

.PP
.PP
.nf
import React from 'react';
import { useMergeRefs } from 'use\-callback\-ref';

const MergedComponent = React\&.forwardRef((props, ref) => {
  const localRef = React\&.useRef();
  // \&.\&.\&.
  // both localRef and ref would be populated with the `ref` to a `div`
  return <div ref={useMergeRefs([localRef, ref])} />;
});
.fi
.PP

.PP
💡 - \fRuseMergeRefs\fP will always give you the same return, and you don't have to worry about \fR[localRef, ref]\fP unique every render\&.
.SS "mergeRefs"
\fRmergeRefs(refs: arrayOfRefs, [defaultValue]):ReactMutableRef\fP - merges a few refs together is a non-hook based version\&. Will produce the new \fRref\fP every run, causing the old one to unmount, and be \fIpopulated\fP with the \fRnull\fP value\&.

.PP
.RS 4
mergeRefs are based on https://github.com/smooth-code/react-merge-refs, just exposes a RefObject, instead of a callback 
.RE
.PP

.PP
\fRmergeRefs\fP are "safe" to use as a part of other hooks-based commands, but don't forget - it returns a new object every call\&.
.SH "Similar packages:"
.PP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRapply-ref\fP - \fRapplyRefs\fP is simular to \fRmergeRef\fP, \fRapplyRef\fP is similar to \fRassignRef\fP
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRuseForkRef\fP - \fRuseForkRef\fP is simular to \fRuseMergeRefs\fP, but accepts only two arguments\&.
.IP "\(bu" 2
\fRreact-merge-refs\fP - \fRmerge-refs\fP is simular to \fRuseMergeRefs\fP, but not a hook and does not provide "stable" reference\&.
.PP

.PP
.PP

.PP
.RS 4
Is it a rocket science? No, \fRRefObject\fP is no more than \fR{current: ref}\fP, and \fRuse-callback-ref\fP is no more than \fRgetter\fP and \fRsetter\fP on that field\&. 
.RE
.PP
